concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "30 Resignation in anticipation of termination of employment"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-30 ;
    values:originalText "(1) This section applies if:   Resignation from the Department  Part 3  Section 30  Australian Border Force Act 2015  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (a) an APS employee in the Department gives the Secretary written notice of the employee’s resignation under paragraph 29(1)(a); and  (b) either:  (i) the Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner reasonably believes that the employee has engaged in serious misconduct and the Secretary is considering terminating the employee’s employment; or  (ii) the employee is being investigated for serious misconduct and the Secretary is not in a position to decide whether to terminate the employee’s employment because the findings of the investigation are not yet known.  (2) The Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner may, by written notice given to the APS employee, substitute a day, no later than 90 days after the day specified in the employee’s notice of resignation, as the day on which the employee’s resignation is to take effect.  (3) Subsection (2) has effect subject to subsection (7). Multiple notices  (4) The Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner may give more than one notice under subsection (2) to the APS employee in relation to the notice of resignation. Timing of first notice  (5) To be effective, the first notice given under subsection (2) in relation to the notice of resignation must be given to the APS employee before the day specified in the notice of resignation." ;
    skos:definition "(1) This section applies if:   Resignation from the Department  Part 3  Section 30  Australian Border Force Act 2015  Compilation date: 04/06/2026  (a) an APS employee in the Department gives the Secretary written notice of the employee’s resignation under paragraph 29(1)(a); and  (b) either:  (i) the Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner reasonably believes that the employee has engaged in serious misconduct and the Secretary is considering terminating the employee’s employment; or  (ii) the employee is being investigated for serious misconduct and the Secretary is not in a position to decide whether to terminate the employee’s employment because the findings of the investigation are not yet known.  (2) The Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner may, by written notice given to the APS employee, substitute a day, no later than 90 days after the day specified in the employee’s notice of resignation, as the day on which the employee’s resignation is to take effect.  (3) Subsection (2) has effect subject to subsection (7). Multiple notices  (4) The Secretary or the Australian Border Force Commissioner may give more than one notice under subsection (2) to the APS employee in relation to the notice of resignation. Timing of first notice  (5) To be effective, the first notice given under subsection (2) in relation to the notice of resignation must be given to the APS employee before the day specified in the notice of resignation."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "b510122eb874e032e5ffac4bd21289742c408e13556e6d675ac4cce91a29048b" ;
    cof:pageNumber "30"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30-ss-4 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-border-force-act-2015-sec-30-ss-5 ;
